### [07:55:00 UTC] Step 3 — Confirm artifact present at shipped default threshold_value=0.6; isolate stage
Hypothesis: The onset elevation is present at the shipped default (not specific to the rejected 0.15 experiment), and originates from IST/cap interaction with the very quiet first WOLA block(s), not LMS warm-up.
Prediction: A synthetic fading broadband signal run through the real pipeline functions at threshold=0.6 should show elevated onset frame-RMS vs a reference envelope; an ablation (with/without IST, with/without cap, with/without LMS) should show which stage dominates.
Experiment: Built a synthetic raised-cosine fade-in (0.5s, various quiet floors) + 5-tone broadband content, ran new_interpolation_algorithm -> iterative_soft_thresholding -> _cap_ist_changes_to_baseline_peak -> autoscale/normalize -> lms_filter -> apply_original_nyquist_cutoff (all real functions, real GPU, threshold=0.6), measured frame-RMS shape deviation vs the true envelope, with ablations.
Analysis: Onset elevation IS present at threshold=0.6 (max ~24.6dB at floor=0.0005). Ablation: interpolation-only ~2.8dB (edge ringing, minor), +LMS-no-IST ~4.1dB, +IST-no-LMS ~24.5dB, full pipeline ~24.65dB -- IST/cap dominates, LMS contributes modestly. Further isolating IST's own WOLA (reflect-padding prototype) only reduced it marginally (24.65->21.07dB), refuting "zero-padded first block" as the dominant mechanism. Directly ablating _cap_ist_changes_to_baseline_peak (uncapped ist_changes vs capped) showed uncapped=2.23dB, capped(shipped)=24.47dB -- decisively isolating the cap's whole-buffer FFT dominant/residual split+shrink as the actual root cause, not WOLA's edge padding nor LMS warm-up (the two coordinator-proposed candidates).
Output: Root cause confirmed: _cap_ist_changes_to_baseline_peak applies a single global-FFT-derived scalar shrink to "dominant" frequency content across the ENTIRE buffer; for a non-stationary (faded) channel this breaks a near-exact dominant/residual cancellation that (uncapped) reconstructs the true quiet onset, unmasking disproportionate residual energy there. Distinct from, though mechanistically similar to (whole-buffer-FFT-on-non-stationary-content), the class of bug iterative_soft_thresholding's own cycle-5 WOLA fix addressed.

### [08:05:00 UTC] Step 4 — Design and verify fix (envelope-gated correction)
Hypothesis: Tapering the cap's dominant-band correction by a smooth local-peak envelope of expanded_channel (full correction near the channel's own peak, ~0 in genuinely quiet regions) will fix the onset artifact while preserving all existing (stationary-signal) cap behavior.
Prediction: On the same synthetic signal, onset deviation should drop sharply; existing cap regression tests (peak-overshoot reduction ~85%, quiet-8000Hz-band survival >90%, no-op cases, safety net, upscale_channels wiring) should still pass since gate~=1 throughout for stationary content.
Experiment: First tried a simpler per-block (non-overlapping-FFT-scope) redesign of the cap itself -- this fixed onset (24.65->4.11dB) but broke quiet-band survival (0.365, need >0.9), a real regression, not a passing test forced to pass. Revised to the envelope-gated correction (new _local_peak_envelope helper reusing existing _wola_block_process machinery; cap now computes the SAME dominant_scale as before but subtracts only `gate * (1-dominant_scale)*dominant_component` instead of applying the raw scalar-band-split globally).
Analysis: Verified on synthetic fading signal: onset deviation 24.65->4.92dB (cap alone), 24.65->6.08dB (full pipeline incl. LMS, floor=0.0005), 11.47->2.49dB (floor=0.01); steady-state deviation ~0dB (matches pre-fix). Re-ran ALL of this function's existing unit tests against the new implementation (manually first, then via the real edited source): peak-overshoot ratio 0.080 (need <0.15, was ~0.077), quiet-band survival 1.014 (need >0.9), no-op cases pass, safety net 0.059 (need <0.15), upscale_channels end-to-end wiring bound satisfied for both stereo channels.
Output: Hypothesis confirmed. Matches prediction exactly.

